# Weakly-linked undefined system symbols that are known to be present in the
# install prefix, and known to be understood.  Anything not listed here fails
# scripts/check-weak-symbols.sh -- see the comment at the top of that script.
#
# A weak reference means the symbol is NULL at runtime on macOS older than the
# version that introduced it.  That is only safe if the caller checks for NULL,
# so every entry below needs a note saying why it is safe (or why we tolerate
# it).  Do not add an entry just to make the build green.
#
# Regenerate the raw list -- as a starting point for triage, not as a fix --
# with:
#
#     scripts/check-weak-symbols.sh "$HOME/gtk/inst" --update
#
# Format: <symbol> <TAB> <system library>

# CPython declares this with `#pragma weak inet_aton` (Modules/socketmodule.c)
# and guards the call with `if (inet_aton != NULL)`, precisely so that a NULL
# resolution is handled.  Safe.
_inet_aton	libSystem

# __OSX_AVAILABLE(15.4), so weak at our deployment target.  gnulib's configure
# finds it declared, links it weakly, and then calls it unguarded -- the same
# bug shape as the glib pipe2 crash.  Tolerated only because the affected
# binaries (bin/bison, bin/cpio) are build-host tools that are not shipped in
# the application bundle.  If either ever ends up in a bundle, patch gnulib
# instead of leaving this entry here.
_strchrnul	libSystem
